  "page1": {
    "title": "Where Does the Wasted Energy Go?",
    "subtitle": "Why no machine is ever perfectly efficient.",
    "body": "Switch on an old light bulb and gently touch it after just a minute. It feels hot, even though its real job is to make light. That heat is a clue to one of the most useful ideas in science: <u>energy transformation</u>. Energy is never created or destroyed; it only changes from one form into another. The electricity flowing into the bulb turns into light, but a large part of it also turns into heat along the way.\n\nThis leftover heat is not really lost, since the total energy stays exactly the same. Still, from our point of view, it is wasted, because we wanted light, not a warm bulb. The fraction of energy that does the job we actually want is called <mark>efficiency</mark>. An old bulb might turn only a tenth of its electricity into light, while a modern LED turns far more. A car engine, too, loses much of its fuel energy as heat instead of motion. No machine, however, ever reaches perfect efficiency, because some energy always escapes as heat, sound, or friction. Even our own bodies feel warm for exactly the same reason.\n\nUnderstanding this idea quietly changes how we build, buy, and choose our everyday tools. Engineers can never break the strict law of conservation, but they can still design clever machines that waste far less and deliver much more. Choosing an efficient device, then, saves you real money and helps protect the whole planet at the very same time. The honest takeaway is both humble and useful: we can never truly get something for nothing, yet with clever and careful design we can get steadily closer to using every last drop of energy on purpose, wasting less and less along the <blank>.",
    "illustration": "../../assets/illustrations/2026-07/18.png",
    "illustration_caption": "Energy changes form but never vanishes; some always leaks away as heat."
  },
